WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.335
Last week we talked about--

00:00:18.335 --> 00:00:19.035
we went quantum.

00:00:19.035 --> 00:00:22.238
We had Dr. Quantum
show us the way.

00:00:22.238 --> 00:00:26.943
And this week, we're going to
go quantum for electrons, right?

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:29.746
That's what we're
going to do this week.

00:00:29.746 --> 00:00:32.649
These are my two favorite
quantum mechanicists

00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:34.250
of all time.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.986
I believe that
this sign is meant

00:00:35.986 --> 00:00:40.056
to teach us something about
the double slit experiment.

00:00:40.056 --> 00:00:42.459
And just as you're
leaving the lot,

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:45.161
knowing that there
is some chance

00:00:45.161 --> 00:00:48.665
that your one car could
actually split into two,

00:00:48.665 --> 00:00:50.967
go through both
exits, and recombine.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.336
Just like an electron
does through two slits.

00:00:53.336 --> 00:00:55.538
And in fact, we know
that that's possible now.

00:00:58.108 --> 00:01:02.512
And if we wanted to, you could
write down-- from de Broglie--

00:01:02.512 --> 00:01:06.016
you could write down like the
wavelength of a car, right?

00:01:06.016 --> 00:01:07.984
So like in a car--

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.553
I don't know, your
lambda of the car

00:01:10.553 --> 00:01:15.091
would be equal to h over mv.

00:01:15.091 --> 00:01:16.860
And if you plus some
numbers in, this

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.795
is going to be something
like oh, you know,

00:01:18.795 --> 00:01:22.032
maybe 10 to the
minus 37th meters.

00:01:22.032 --> 00:01:25.368
It's small, but it's a wave.

00:01:25.368 --> 00:01:28.170
Your car is a wave, you know?

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.374
The baseball is a wave.

00:01:31.374 --> 00:01:34.377
The electron-- we are all waves.

00:01:34.377 --> 00:01:37.881
And I know, and that's what--
so the electron is a wave.

00:01:37.881 --> 00:01:41.116
And I know-- hashtag,
we are all waves.

00:01:41.116 --> 00:01:43.019
Because it's a thing.

00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:44.253
It's really a thing.

00:01:44.253 --> 00:01:46.622
Because it's true.

00:01:46.622 --> 00:01:48.491
I am a wave.

00:01:48.491 --> 00:01:49.526
You are a wave.

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:50.894
We are all waves.

00:01:50.894 --> 00:01:52.762
It's just-- our wavelengths
are pretty small,

00:01:52.762 --> 00:01:54.330
but they're not zero.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.433
And so the next time
you get-- oh, we're

00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:57.600
on the same wavelength.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.836
You really are.

00:01:59.836 --> 00:02:01.037
It's true.

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:02.172
We are-- right?

00:02:02.172 --> 00:02:03.640
That's deep.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.608
We are waves.

00:02:05.608 --> 00:02:06.543
All of us.

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:09.112
And so, the wavelength--

00:02:09.112 --> 00:02:11.448
OK, the wavelength for an
electron was something like 10

00:02:11.448 --> 00:02:13.917
to the minus 10th meters.

00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:18.621
Which made it a really nice
way to interrogate images.

00:02:18.621 --> 00:02:21.057
And that was my "why this
matters" last week, right?

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:21.858
On Friday.

00:02:21.858 --> 00:02:26.863
So you can take advantage of
that wavelength to do things.

00:02:26.863 --> 00:02:31.034
Like see electron
microscopy, right?

00:02:31.034 --> 00:02:32.535
But everyone's awake.

00:02:32.535 --> 00:02:36.239
That's what quantum
mechanics told us.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:37.941
But what we want
to do now is know

00:02:37.941 --> 00:02:41.845
how to represent this wave.

00:02:41.845 --> 00:02:43.446
So everything's waving.

00:02:43.446 --> 00:02:44.447
We've got that.

00:02:44.447 --> 00:02:46.683
But how do I describe it?

00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:47.216
Right?

00:02:47.216 --> 00:02:49.586
And that's where we left off.

00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:51.353
And we left off
by me telling you

00:02:51.353 --> 00:02:55.658
that it all led to Schrodinger,
who came up with the wave

00:02:55.658 --> 00:02:57.460
equation for quantum mechanics.

00:02:57.527 --> 00:03:00.630
And the wave equation
of Schrodinger

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.767
is really pretty
incredible, because--

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:07.103
so I'm not going to
write out Schrodinger.

00:03:07.103 --> 00:03:08.605
That would take me forever.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:13.076
So I'm going to
write S. Eq., right?

00:03:13.076 --> 00:03:16.012
The Schrodinger equation, right?

00:03:16.012 --> 00:03:23.887
And here-- so what he had is he
had the sort of wave behavior

00:03:23.887 --> 00:03:25.788
that was captured.

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:27.690
And it was captured
automatically.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.694
And it gave us also
allowed energies, you see?

00:03:31.694 --> 00:03:34.664
So, allowed energies.

00:03:34.664 --> 00:03:40.203
And this is also
the quantization.

00:03:40.203 --> 00:03:41.938
Ah-- I ran out of room.

00:03:41.938 --> 00:03:42.438
Right.

00:03:42.438 --> 00:03:44.941
But now, If you go
back to classical--

00:03:44.941 --> 00:03:49.045
so if you think about
classical physics, what

00:03:49.045 --> 00:03:51.614
you would have is something
like maybe F equals ma.

00:03:51.614 --> 00:03:54.517
But what that gives you--

00:03:54.517 --> 00:03:56.853
well, it doesn't give
you wave behavior,

00:03:56.853 --> 00:04:01.157
but it gives you
continuous energies.

00:04:01.157 --> 00:04:05.361
Continuous energies.

00:04:05.361 --> 00:04:10.466
So the powerful point here
is that from that equation--

00:04:10.466 --> 00:04:13.236
and we don't need to
know this equation.

00:04:13.236 --> 00:04:14.637
We're not going to derive it.

00:04:14.637 --> 00:04:15.138
Right?

00:04:15.138 --> 00:04:16.973
Today I'm going to tell
you how it's solved,

00:04:16.973 --> 00:04:18.241
but you don't need to
know how to solve it.

00:04:18.241 --> 00:04:20.543
But you need to know
what the solutions mean.

00:04:20.543 --> 00:04:22.512
That's what we're
talking about today.

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:29.052
But that equation-- what it
does is it sets up the energies

00:04:29.052 --> 00:04:30.987
as a function of the wave.

00:04:30.987 --> 00:04:36.192
Psi-- that function, psi--
is the wave function, OK?

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:39.128
And so you get waves by default,
because that's what you're

00:04:39.128 --> 00:04:40.897
solving for-- these waves.

00:04:40.897 --> 00:04:42.665
And then you have
a minus second--

00:04:42.665 --> 00:04:44.701
oh, there's an h bar.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.803
That's an h over 2 pi.

00:04:49.239 --> 00:04:49.739
Right?

00:04:49.739 --> 00:04:52.642
But it's like the same
Planck's constant.

00:04:52.642 --> 00:04:53.142
OK.

00:04:53.142 --> 00:04:56.179
But the wave
behavior gives you--

00:04:56.179 --> 00:04:58.748
because there's only
certain energies that

00:04:58.748 --> 00:04:59.782
solve that equation.

00:04:59.782 --> 00:05:03.720
So quantization
falls out naturally.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.655
You don't have to
put it in anymore.

00:05:05.655 --> 00:05:09.659
It is a direct result of
solving this equation.

00:05:09.659 --> 00:05:11.828
If I take a wave--

00:05:11.828 --> 00:05:12.862
think about this, right.

00:05:12.862 --> 00:05:16.266
If you take a wave and
you put it in a box,

00:05:16.266 --> 00:05:19.569
then it can only have
certain solutions.

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:23.506
Once you define the box,
if you throw a wave in it,

00:05:23.506 --> 00:05:27.043
well, that's where those
things are on the right are.

00:05:27.043 --> 00:05:28.511
Maybe it's a standing wave.

00:05:28.511 --> 00:05:30.913
And so it's going to look--

00:05:30.913 --> 00:05:35.752
no-- it's going to be like this.

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:37.754
One solution.

00:05:37.754 --> 00:05:41.624
And some energy.

00:05:41.624 --> 00:05:44.193
Or maybe it's going to look--

00:05:44.193 --> 00:05:46.162
I didn't leave myself any room--

00:05:46.162 --> 00:05:48.464
like this.

00:05:48.464 --> 00:05:51.501
And it's going to have
some other energy, right?

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:52.902
1, 2.

00:05:52.902 --> 00:05:53.403
That's it.

00:05:53.403 --> 00:05:54.937
If you throw a wave
in a box-- like,

00:05:54.937 --> 00:05:56.539
here's the first
one, second one.

00:05:56.539 --> 00:05:59.642
But there are only certain
ones that are going to work.

00:05:59.642 --> 00:06:01.844
All right, and you
can get that sense.

00:06:01.844 --> 00:06:04.013
Because otherwise it's not
going to line up and then

00:06:04.013 --> 00:06:06.616
be able to wave and keep going.

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:08.651
It's not going to
be a standing wave.

00:06:08.651 --> 00:06:09.552
Right?

00:06:09.552 --> 00:06:12.422
So you get this sense
right away of quantization.

00:06:12.422 --> 00:06:15.358
Only certain waves, and
only certain energies,

00:06:15.358 --> 00:06:16.559
will solve this equation.

00:06:16.559 --> 00:06:20.830
Now, here's the key, is this--

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:24.400
we're going to throw an electron
into this equation, right?

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.637
But we're going to throw it
into the right potential.

00:06:27.637 --> 00:06:31.207
And that is the potential that
we know it sits in, in an atom.

00:06:31.207 --> 00:06:34.377
Which is simply a
Coulomb potential.

00:06:34.377 --> 00:06:36.245
So this is how we
get there, right?

00:06:36.245 --> 00:06:39.549
We take Schrodinger's
equation of quantum mechanics.

00:06:39.549 --> 00:06:45.855
And we solve it for an
electron waving in the presence

00:06:45.855 --> 00:06:49.225
of the potential of the atom.

00:06:49.225 --> 00:06:51.527
Which is a positive potential.

00:06:51.527 --> 00:06:52.695
And you can see, this is a--

00:06:52.695 --> 00:06:54.497
OK, V is a potential.

00:06:54.497 --> 00:06:56.999
And that's an attractive
potential, right?

00:06:56.999 --> 00:06:58.735
That's an attractive potential.

00:06:58.735 --> 00:07:00.069
So what happens when we do that?

00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:01.437
So that's what
we're going to do.

00:07:01.437 --> 00:07:03.072
I'm going to leave this for--

00:07:03.072 --> 00:07:03.906
so.

00:07:03.906 --> 00:07:04.440
What happens?

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.810
So if we take a hydrogen atom--

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:14.517
or as I've written it here, a
"Hatom," then you would have--

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:15.017
Let's see.

00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:16.085
What do you get from this?

00:07:16.085 --> 00:07:20.757
Well, you get that the potential
is going to be minus 1 over r.

00:07:20.757 --> 00:07:21.257
Yeah.

00:07:21.257 --> 00:07:22.625
It goes as minus 1--

00:07:22.625 --> 00:07:26.095
an e and an over-permittivity
of space and stuff like that.

00:07:26.095 --> 00:07:28.898
But it goes as minus 1 over r.

00:07:28.898 --> 00:07:31.134
That's the Coulomb potential.

00:07:31.134 --> 00:07:32.168
Right?

00:07:32.168 --> 00:07:39.242
A plus charge is sitting
outside of a minus charge.

00:07:39.242 --> 00:07:41.344
Ah!

00:07:41.344 --> 00:07:42.779
That was a close call.

00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:47.016
The electron is sitting out
here, and there's the nucleus.

00:07:47.016 --> 00:07:49.152
And the potential feels
is a Coulomb potential.

00:07:49.152 --> 00:07:50.486
OK, so that's point 1.

00:07:50.486 --> 00:07:56.558
Point 2 is that we're
going to apply the--

00:07:56.558 --> 00:07:59.662
oh, I'm saving
myself so much time.

00:07:59.662 --> 00:08:02.064
I love it, when you're
efficient like that--

00:08:02.064 --> 00:08:02.832
S. Eq.

00:08:02.832 --> 00:08:04.801
Schrodinger equation.

00:08:04.801 --> 00:08:06.569
The Schrodinger equation.

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:09.972
But the way that we're going
to do this is in spherical

00:08:09.972 --> 00:08:13.109
coordinates.

00:08:13.109 --> 00:08:17.680
Spherical coords.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.283
And all that means
is that I'm going

00:08:20.283 --> 00:08:29.091
to write psi as a function
of r, theta, and phi.

00:08:32.462 --> 00:08:35.264
And the last thing
that I'm going to do

00:08:35.264 --> 00:08:39.635
is I'm going to
separate the variables.

00:08:39.635 --> 00:08:42.772
And that's just a
standard way to solve

00:08:42.772 --> 00:08:43.940
mathematical equations.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.575
So I'm going to
separate the variables.

00:08:45.575 --> 00:08:47.076
So separate.

00:08:47.076 --> 00:08:54.317
And so what that means is
that psi can be written as one

00:08:54.317 --> 00:08:56.919
function-- let's call it r--

00:08:56.919 --> 00:09:03.726
of the radius, times another
function, p, of the theta,

00:09:03.726 --> 00:09:06.262
times another function f of phi.

00:09:06.262 --> 00:09:09.932
So that's my separation
of variables, OK?

00:09:09.932 --> 00:09:13.703
Again, I'm not all
about having you know

00:09:13.703 --> 00:09:16.706
how to solve this equation.

00:09:16.706 --> 00:09:17.540
OK?

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.942
That you can learn
in quantum mechanics.

00:09:19.942 --> 00:09:23.779
But what I'm all about is what
that quantum mechanics solution

00:09:23.779 --> 00:09:26.415
means for us here in chemistry.

00:09:26.415 --> 00:09:28.351
And this is deep.

00:09:28.351 --> 00:09:29.585
This is deep, because--

00:09:29.585 --> 00:09:30.319
why?

00:09:30.319 --> 00:09:35.057
Because this whole thing
is quantized, right?

00:09:35.057 --> 00:09:43.199
So just like before, there's
only certain allowed values

00:09:43.199 --> 00:09:44.500
for the wave function.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:50.473
There's only certain allowed
values for these variables.

00:09:50.473 --> 00:09:53.242
Each one of them is quantized.

00:09:53.242 --> 00:09:55.144
Each one of them.

00:09:55.144 --> 00:09:56.078
OK?

00:09:56.078 --> 00:09:58.347
And that's what we need
to learn about today,

00:09:58.347 --> 00:10:00.716
is what that means
for chemistry.

00:10:00.716 --> 00:10:01.951
All right.

00:10:01.951 --> 00:10:08.357
So now we're going to go
through the quantum numbers

00:10:08.357 --> 00:10:09.325
and see what they mean.

00:10:09.325 --> 00:10:12.295
And we're going to start
with this first one,

00:10:12.295 --> 00:10:14.997
the radial function.

00:10:14.997 --> 00:10:19.402
So radial, and then
two angular functions.

00:10:19.402 --> 00:10:20.069
OK.

00:10:20.069 --> 00:10:22.438
Now the radial--
so what do we get?

00:10:22.438 --> 00:10:26.075
Well, the radial function--

00:10:26.075 --> 00:10:28.544
I'm using a word here,
I got to tell you why.

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:31.547
Orbital of hydrogen, right?

00:10:31.547 --> 00:10:32.682
So I've solved this now.

00:10:32.682 --> 00:10:38.688
I've solved this equation,
and I'm plotting this orbital

00:10:38.688 --> 00:10:42.825
for first quantization--
n equals 1, n equals 2,

00:10:42.825 --> 00:10:43.759
n equals 3.

00:10:43.759 --> 00:10:45.728
We've got to talk
about this, right?

00:10:45.728 --> 00:10:49.832
But I'm just plotting what
that function looks like.

00:10:49.832 --> 00:10:51.233
Well, let's compare.

00:10:51.233 --> 00:10:56.339
Because if I'm Bohr,
then what I have is this.

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:56.906
Remember this?

00:10:56.906 --> 00:10:58.407
I've got like this--

00:10:58.407 --> 00:11:01.744
OK, there's 1, and
it's going around,

00:11:01.744 --> 00:11:04.347
and it's very happy in an orbit.

00:11:08.784 --> 00:11:12.355
But see, we don't call
these orbits anymore.

00:11:12.355 --> 00:11:14.724
And it's going to be clear as
we talk about this picture,

00:11:14.724 --> 00:11:18.728
because these aren't
distinct orbits, right?

00:11:18.728 --> 00:11:26.068
So now in quantum
what you have--

00:11:26.068 --> 00:11:29.238
I'm just going to take
that n equals 1 solution.

00:11:29.238 --> 00:11:34.443
That's the first solution
to the radial part, right?

00:11:34.443 --> 00:11:36.879
That's not a fixed distance.

00:11:36.879 --> 00:11:38.914
That's not a fixed
distance, right?

00:11:38.914 --> 00:11:44.153
So now I'm just looking
at the radial distribution

00:11:44.153 --> 00:11:46.455
of the probability.

00:11:46.455 --> 00:11:49.759
The probability of
finding the electron.

00:11:49.759 --> 00:11:53.129
That's how we speak
when we speak quantum.

00:11:53.129 --> 00:11:55.998
We don't say it's here, right?

00:11:55.998 --> 00:11:58.034
We say it could be here.

00:11:58.034 --> 00:11:58.567
I don't know.

00:11:58.567 --> 00:12:00.336
But there's a probability
that it's there.

00:12:00.336 --> 00:12:05.975
So now it sort of has
a probability cloud.

00:12:05.975 --> 00:12:09.245
Now it looks like it
gets a little less--

00:12:09.245 --> 00:12:11.213
why am I drawing spirals?

00:12:11.213 --> 00:12:13.549
It looks like it gets
a little less probable

00:12:13.549 --> 00:12:15.551
as you go further out, right?

00:12:15.551 --> 00:12:17.753
So maybe the density of
probability-- because look,

00:12:17.753 --> 00:12:18.254
right?

00:12:18.254 --> 00:12:19.889
It's got a peak there.

00:12:19.889 --> 00:12:25.261
But even if I just plot
that first orbital,

00:12:25.261 --> 00:12:30.232
even as I go all the way out
to here, it is non-0, right?

00:12:30.232 --> 00:12:32.468
So this is probability.

00:12:37.139 --> 00:12:39.241
And this would be n equals 1.

00:12:39.241 --> 00:12:41.977
So it's a totally
different story.

00:12:41.977 --> 00:12:46.949
That electron isn't in
one place, but it is.

00:12:46.949 --> 00:12:48.451
But it's not.

00:12:48.451 --> 00:12:49.585
It could be anywhere.

00:12:49.585 --> 00:12:51.854
It could be-- if
I'm a hydrogen atom,

00:12:51.854 --> 00:12:54.457
my electron could literally
be across the room.

00:12:54.457 --> 00:12:57.693
There is a finite
probability that that's true.

00:12:57.693 --> 00:13:01.430
And we know that since
we are all waves,

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:04.567
and we can all be defined
by the equations of quantum

00:13:04.567 --> 00:13:08.137
mechanics-- and in fact, that
is the way to define the world--

00:13:08.137 --> 00:13:11.040
that we also have probabilities.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.375
They're small, you know.

00:13:13.375 --> 00:13:16.178
But I could be over
there right now.

00:13:16.178 --> 00:13:17.346
There's a chance.

00:13:17.346 --> 00:13:20.216
My weight function
is not exactly 0

00:13:20.216 --> 00:13:21.317
all the way over there.

00:13:21.317 --> 00:13:22.451
It's close.

00:13:22.451 --> 00:13:26.155
But it's not exact, right?

00:13:26.155 --> 00:13:30.793
That is a very different picture
of an electron in an orbit--

00:13:30.793 --> 00:13:32.261
in fact, it's not an orbit.

00:13:32.261 --> 00:13:34.163
It's an orbital.

00:13:34.163 --> 00:13:39.034
And that's where we say orbital.

00:13:39.034 --> 00:13:39.935
That's why we stay it.

00:13:39.935 --> 00:13:42.438
OK, but-- we're listing
quantum numbers.

00:13:42.438 --> 00:13:44.707
These are the quantum numbers.

00:13:44.707 --> 00:13:49.445
Oh, oh- I'm going all caps.

00:13:49.445 --> 00:13:52.581
Didn't even know I
was going to do that.

00:13:52.581 --> 00:13:55.618
So we have n, which is
called the principle--

00:13:58.754 --> 00:14:04.660
