as with creating images

00:19:41.797 --> 00:19:44.299
and establishing
mental connections."

00:19:44.299 --> 00:19:45.801
That's Bohr like
deep in it, trying

00:19:45.801 --> 00:19:48.570
to figure out what psi means.

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:49.872
He's like, I don't know.

00:19:49.872 --> 00:19:52.741
It's poetry.

00:19:52.741 --> 00:19:55.010
OK, that's cool.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:56.879
That's cool.

00:19:56.879 --> 00:19:58.547
V squared means
something, v doesn't.

00:19:58.547 --> 00:19:59.615
Really?

00:19:59.615 --> 00:20:00.549
No.

00:20:00.549 --> 00:20:02.951
Classically, we've got nothing.

00:20:02.951 --> 00:20:03.785
Right?

00:20:03.785 --> 00:20:04.887
Quantum mechanics.

00:20:04.887 --> 00:20:06.388
Oh, it's a beautiful thing.

00:20:06.388 --> 00:20:07.289
Why does this matter?

00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:09.324
I'm going to give you a
brief "why this matters."

00:20:09.324 --> 00:20:12.561
And we've been talking about
the quantum nature of things,

00:20:12.561 --> 00:20:13.328
right?

00:20:13.328 --> 00:20:14.830
Quantum is this.

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:16.532
Quantum are these differences.

00:20:16.532 --> 00:20:17.933
It's this probability.

00:20:17.933 --> 00:20:20.669
It's quantization of energies.

00:20:20.669 --> 00:20:22.371
What does that mean, right?

00:20:22.371 --> 00:20:25.774
Well, last time I told
you that by electrons

00:20:25.774 --> 00:20:28.243
being quantum, and having
wave-like character--

00:20:28.243 --> 00:20:30.445
we're able to use
them to see things,

00:20:30.445 --> 00:20:32.814
and it started the
nanotechnology revolution

00:20:32.814 --> 00:20:34.116
about 30 years ago.

00:20:34.116 --> 00:20:36.251
And today I want to give
you an example of that

00:20:36.251 --> 00:20:38.954
in my "why this matters."

00:20:38.954 --> 00:20:42.691
And it has to do with quantum
domination, which should be

00:20:42.691 --> 00:20:46.495
the title of a movie or a book.

00:20:46.495 --> 00:20:49.064
And here's the example-- take
a piece of bulk material.

00:20:49.064 --> 00:20:50.999
Take a piece of silicon, OK?

00:20:50.999 --> 00:20:52.668
And if you look at
the silicon, it's

00:20:52.668 --> 00:20:54.303
kind of boring to
look at optically.

00:20:54.303 --> 00:20:55.704
It's not very interesting.

00:20:55.704 --> 00:20:57.406
You know, don't tell
that to your iPhone,

00:20:57.406 --> 00:21:00.943
which has a lot of it in
there, but it's boring.

00:21:00.943 --> 00:21:04.613
But now, take out your little
nano ice cream scooper,

00:21:04.613 --> 00:21:05.514
which you all have.

00:21:05.514 --> 00:21:07.482
And you take a little
tiny piece of it.

00:21:07.482 --> 00:21:10.052
We call that a quantum dot.

00:21:10.052 --> 00:21:10.752
Why?

00:21:10.752 --> 00:21:13.322
We call it a quantum dot
because it kind of looks

00:21:13.322 --> 00:21:15.958
like a dot-- its smallish.

00:21:15.958 --> 00:21:18.560
But we call it that
because quantum mechanics

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:20.362
takes over the properties.

00:21:20.362 --> 00:21:23.732
Here's an example-- shine
light on this thing,

00:21:23.732 --> 00:21:25.834
or have it glow, and
all of a sudden instead

00:21:25.834 --> 00:21:29.805
of being boring it can
be any color you want.

00:21:29.805 --> 00:21:30.572
Why?

00:21:30.572 --> 00:21:32.307
Because if I shine
light-- and here is

00:21:32.307 --> 00:21:36.011
a very sophisticated picture
of a laser, which you can tell

00:21:36.011 --> 00:21:38.447
I had trouble
making transparent.

00:21:38.447 --> 00:21:41.750
And so you shine a
laser on this piece--

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:46.288
we know already, light
excites charge in an atom.

00:21:46.288 --> 00:21:47.623
It does the same in solids.

00:21:47.623 --> 00:21:50.726
So here's my piece of
silicon, and what that does

00:21:50.726 --> 00:21:53.862
is it sends an electron
up in energy levels.

00:21:53.862 --> 00:21:55.631
But the electron is
up in an energy level,

00:21:55.631 --> 00:21:57.432
and it left behind a hole.

00:21:57.432 --> 00:21:59.334
Which is something we'll
talk about later when

00:21:59.334 --> 00:22:01.236
we talk about semiconductors.

00:22:01.236 --> 00:22:04.072
A hole is just a
positive charge.

00:22:04.072 --> 00:22:06.875
And it turns out that that
electron and that hole

00:22:06.875 --> 00:22:11.313
are attracted to each other, but
not-- they can't get too close.

00:22:11.313 --> 00:22:13.882
But they want to kind of hang
out at a certain distance.

00:22:13.882 --> 00:22:14.383
Where?

00:22:14.383 --> 00:22:15.584
How far?

00:22:15.584 --> 00:22:18.153
Yeah-- quantum mechanics.

00:22:18.153 --> 00:22:21.123
N 2, 3, 4 tells us.

00:22:21.123 --> 00:22:24.626
It tells us how far, right?

00:22:24.626 --> 00:22:25.594
They want to hang out--

00:22:25.594 --> 00:22:28.063
and now all of a sudden,
I've nano-scooped them out,

00:22:28.063 --> 00:22:29.231
and I've made a quantum dot.

00:22:29.231 --> 00:22:31.099
But if I try to do
the same thing there,

00:22:31.099 --> 00:22:33.001
that's how far
they wanted to be.

00:22:33.001 --> 00:22:33.935
And they can't be.

00:22:33.935 --> 00:22:36.972
They literally ran
out of real estate.

00:22:36.972 --> 00:22:38.940
They ran out of atoms.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:41.743
They cannot be out here, because
there's no stuff out here.

00:22:41.743 --> 00:22:43.745
So they get squeezed.

00:22:43.745 --> 00:22:46.415
I am squeezing these
quantum mechanical objects,

00:22:46.415 --> 00:22:48.116
this electron and this hole.

00:22:48.116 --> 00:22:52.487
And by squeezing them,
I'm changing the way

00:22:52.487 --> 00:22:53.855
they interact with light.

00:22:53.855 --> 00:22:57.659
I'm changing the color, right?

00:22:57.659 --> 00:22:58.560
Oh.

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.163
It's all stuff we've
learned that explains

00:23:01.163 --> 00:23:03.765
something revolutionary.

00:23:03.765 --> 00:23:07.102
By the way, if you buy a QLED
TV, is it as good as OLED?

00:23:07.102 --> 00:23:08.036
No.

00:23:08.036 --> 00:23:09.805
But is it better than LED?

00:23:09.805 --> 00:23:10.939
Yeah, a little bit.

00:23:10.939 --> 00:23:12.040
That's what they-- QLED--

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:14.142
quantum is quantum dots.

00:23:14.142 --> 00:23:17.913
They have coated the LED
emitters with quantum dots

00:23:17.913 --> 00:23:20.349
to give you better color.

00:23:20.349 --> 00:23:24.386
Now what this means,
why this matters,

00:23:24.386 --> 00:23:27.089
is nothing less than the
periodic table itself.

00:23:27.089 --> 00:23:29.658
Because I think I
showed you this--

00:23:29.658 --> 00:23:31.893
we're living in these
different ages, which I love.

00:23:31.893 --> 00:23:33.995
We have the periodic
table that has--

00:23:33.995 --> 00:23:36.498
the ability to work with
it is what's brought us

00:23:36.498 --> 00:23:39.634
to the age of materials design.

00:23:39.634 --> 00:23:44.706
But see, now I've literally
just told you that by changing

00:23:44.706 --> 00:23:47.042
the size-- nothing
more than the size--

00:23:47.042 --> 00:23:50.045
I can tune a property
because of quantum mechanics.

00:23:50.045 --> 00:23:52.748
Because I'm changing its
quantum mechanical interactions.

00:23:52.748 --> 00:23:56.218
It would be like saying, I can
take a piece of this table--

00:23:56.218 --> 00:23:57.686
I can break a piece
of this table,

00:23:57.686 --> 00:24:00.088
and it's now going to be red.

00:24:00.088 --> 00:24:03.425
That's exactly what we're
doing, but at a very tiny size.

00:24:03.425 --> 00:24:06.795
That is as if we are
taking this periodic table

00:24:06.795 --> 00:24:09.798
and giving it a whole
other dimension.

00:24:09.798 --> 00:24:14.236
Every element can do more
things than we thought possible

00:24:14.236 --> 00:24:17.372
because we can tune
these properties related

00:24:17.372 --> 00:24:19.408
to quantum mechanics.

00:24:19.408 --> 00:24:20.876
So that's a big deal.

00:24:20.876 --> 00:24:22.477
That's a very big deal.

00:24:22.477 --> 00:24:22.978
OK.

00:24:22.978 --> 00:24:26.848
Now back to our solution to
the Schrodinger equation.

00:24:26.848 --> 00:24:29.151
For an electron in an atom.

00:24:29.151 --> 00:24:36.458
Because now we move on and
we solve for the next one,

00:24:36.458 --> 00:24:37.492
which is--

00:24:37.492 --> 00:24:39.494
OK, so we did r.

00:24:39.494 --> 00:24:42.030
Now we're going to do theta.

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:44.299
And what happens
with theta is, you

00:24:44.299 --> 00:24:48.737
get another quantization
of that variable.

00:24:48.737 --> 00:24:50.639
And that's the second
quantum number.

00:24:50.639 --> 00:24:51.907
So we're going to put it here.

00:24:51.907 --> 00:24:56.244
N-- I'll number them
so we don't lose track.

00:24:56.244 --> 00:24:59.548
Who knows how many they'll be?

00:24:59.548 --> 00:25:01.082
OK.

00:25:01.082 --> 00:25:03.418
Not three.

00:25:03.418 --> 00:25:07.322
OK, so the second one is l.

00:25:07.322 --> 00:25:12.093
And it's the angular
quantum number.

00:25:15.163 --> 00:25:23.271
And it has also
quantization 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:25:23.271 --> 00:25:23.772
OK?

00:25:23.772 --> 00:25:26.641
So l can be--

00:25:26.641 --> 00:25:29.077
Oh, I need to put that up there.

00:25:29.077 --> 00:25:31.246
I meant to put that right here.

00:25:31.246 --> 00:25:35.383
This is just like what we
learned for Bohr, right?

00:25:38.753 --> 00:25:46.761
And l can be 0, 1, 2, all
the way up to l minus 1.

00:25:46.761 --> 00:25:48.830
OK?

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:52.534
I'm going too fast,
that's n minus 1.

00:25:52.534 --> 00:25:55.136
L goes from 0 to n minus 1.

00:25:57.939 --> 00:26:01.877
OK, so what that
means right away--

00:26:01.877 --> 00:26:03.879
what you can see-- if it
only goes to n minus 1,

00:26:03.879 --> 00:26:10.986
then for example for n
equals 1 then l equals 0.

00:26:10.986 --> 00:26:12.554
That's it.

00:26:12.554 --> 00:26:14.089
There's no other options.

00:26:14.089 --> 00:26:16.091
It stops, right?

00:26:16.091 --> 00:26:17.626
And we have a name for this.

00:26:17.626 --> 00:26:19.427
We call this s, the s orbital.

00:26:23.198 --> 00:26:31.473
And for n equals 2, l
can be 0, or l can be 1.

00:26:31.473 --> 00:26:34.109
And this one is called--

00:26:34.109 --> 00:26:36.211
this one is the p orbital.

00:26:36.211 --> 00:26:42.384
This is the p orbital, OK?

00:26:42.384 --> 00:26:43.184
Those have names.

00:26:43.184 --> 00:26:45.353
And so on and so on.

00:26:45.353 --> 00:26:48.456
d, f--

00:26:48.456 --> 00:26:53.261
This is old notation that
we cannot get rid of.

00:26:53.261 --> 00:26:55.063
This is called
spectroscopic notation,

00:26:55.063 --> 00:26:56.865
because it comes
from spectroscopists.

00:26:56.865 --> 00:27:00.435
And remember, I told you-- never
make a spectroscopist angry.

00:27:00.435 --> 00:27:01.970
Don't do it.

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:03.138
Don't do it.

00:27:03.138 --> 00:27:05.340
And so we use their
notation still.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:06.741
s was sharp.

00:27:06.741 --> 00:27:08.577
Spherically symmetric.

00:27:08.577 --> 00:27:10.045
p was principle,
because it sort of

00:27:10.045 --> 00:27:12.414
dominated in some experiments.
d was more diffuse.

00:27:12.414 --> 00:27:15.050
f fundamental, because it looked
kind of like the hydrogen atom

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:16.518
sometimes.

00:27:16.518 --> 00:27:19.054
These shouldn't be the names,
but they are because they still

00:27:19.054 --> 00:27:20.422
stay with us.

00:27:20.422 --> 00:27:23.458
Spectroscopic
notation-- s, p, d, f.

00:27:23.458 --> 00:27:26.494
What they really do
is just correspond--

00:27:26.494 --> 00:27:27.929
that looks like "porbital."

00:27:30.565 --> 00:27:32.567
Let's put a little
more space there.

00:27:32.567 --> 00:27:34.336
P orbital.

00:27:34.336 --> 00:27:35.170
Right?

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:38.340
Because what they really
mean is nothing more

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:40.976
than a quantum number for the
second quantum number, which

00:27:40.976 --> 00:27:43.445
is the angular quantum number.

00:27:43.445 --> 00:27:46.881
These are also sometimes
called subshells, right?

00:27:46.881 --> 00:27:50.819
Because they're
underneath the shell.

00:27:50.819 --> 00:27:53.088
And as you can see
from this picture,

00:27:53.088 --> 00:27:58.059
it describes the angular shape.

00:27:58.059 --> 00:28:01.963
The angular shape
of the orbital.

00:28:01.963 --> 00:28:03.164
The shape, right?

00:28:03.164 --> 00:28:04.099
So it gives us a shape.

00:28:08.069 --> 00:28:16.678
Now-- if we think about it, and
we think about that p orbital,

00:28:16.678 --> 00:28:19.648
that has a different shape
than the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:28:19.648 --> 00:28:22.450
Then you think, well,
this could be aligned

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:24.819
in different directions, right?

00:28:24.819 --> 00:28:26.588
So if I think about
that p orbital-- well,

00:28:26.588 --> 00:28:29.057
I could align it in
three different ways.

00:28:29.057 --> 00:28:33.461
It could go along this axis,
this axis, or that one, right?

00:28:33.461 --> 00:28:36.564
And so those are p
orbitals in hydrogen

00:28:36.564 --> 00:28:39.367
that have three
possible orientations,

00:28:39.367 --> 00:28:43.371
and that automatically gets
us to another quantum number.

00:28:43.371 --> 00:28:46.441
In fact, it's the last
one, and it makes sense

00:28:46.441 --> 00:28:49.611
just physically--
that's phi, right?

00:28:49.611 --> 00:28:52.213
So the last one--

00:28:52.213 --> 00:28:53.915
or is it the last one--

00:28:53.915 --> 00:28:54.949
is m.

00:28:54.949 --> 00:29:02.457
And we call it m sub l, which
is the magnetic quantum number.

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:11.800
And this one can have
quantization equal to minus l,

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:12.300
right?

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:15.937
All the way up to plus l.

00:29:15.937 --> 00:29:22.977
So the number of orientations
that the orbital can have--

00:29:22.977 --> 00:29:31.986
if I'm a-- n equals
2, l equals 1,

00:29:31.986 --> 00:29:38.326
then m sub l equals
minus 1, 0, 1.

00:29:38.326 --> 00:29:40.261
Three orientations.

00:29:40.261 --> 00:29:40.762
Right?

00:29:40.762 --> 00:29:44.499
It's got three orientations--
three possible magnetic quantum

00:29:44.499 --> 00:29:44.999
numbers.

00:29:44.999 --> 00:29:46.835
Why is it called the
magnetic quantum number?

00:29:46.835 --> 00:29:50.972
Because those are
the experiments.

00:29:50.972 --> 00:29:53.975
We're done using magnetization--

00:29:53.975 --> 00:29:58.246
magnetic fields--
to discover these

00:29:58.246 --> 00:30:00.548
orientations, to
sort through it.

00:30:00.548 --> 00:30:02.317
To see it.

00:30:02.317 --> 00:30:03.017
OK.

00:30:03.017 --> 00:30:04.953
So if I had l equals--

00:30:04.953 --> 00:30:10.291
all right, so if I had,
for example l equals 2.

00:30:10.291 --> 00:30:13.495
Then m sub l would
have five values.

00:30:13.495 --> 00:30:16.030
Five possible values, right?

00:30:18.733 --> 00:30:20.902
I should put a plus
there, just to be sure we

00:30:20.902 --> 00:30:22.637
keep track of minus and plus.

00:30:22.637 --> 00:30:25.473
These are quantum numbers.

00:30:25.473 --> 00:30:27.075
That means they're
integers, right?

00:30:27.075 --> 00:30:32.947
But they can have these ranges
and these rules, and again--

00:30:32.947 --> 00:30:35.350
you may think, why did
this come out like this?

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:39.354
Well, it comes out from
solving this equation--

00:30:39.354 --> 00:30:41.723
separation of variables--

00:30:41.723 --> 00:30:42.824
with that potential.

00:30:42.824 --> 00:30:44.793
That's it, right?

00:30:44.793 --> 00:30:45.293
That's it.

00:30:45.293 --> 00:30:49.931
You get these allowed values of
these three variables, quantum

00:30:49.931 --> 00:30:50.698
numbers.

00:30:50.698 --> 00:30:51.499
OK?

00:30:51.499 --> 00:30:51.966
All right.

00:30:56.137 --> 00:30:57.205
Now-- and there's the d.

00:30:57.205 --> 00:30:59.140
These are called d--

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:03.211
So if n equals 2--

00:31:03.211 --> 00:31:03.711
OK.

00:31:03.711 --> 00:31:07.148
So did I mess up here?

00:31:07.148 --> 00:31:12.320
No, I didn't, because n would
have to be 3 here, right?

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:13.988
For l to be 2.

00:31:13.988 --> 00:31:17.158
Because l can't be equal to
n, because I wrote it there

00:31:17.158 --> 00:31:21.296
as the list of allowed quantum
numbers that l can have.

00:31:21.296 --> 00:31:24.699
And there it is
for the d orbitals.

00:31:24.699 --> 00:31:26.901
And so there's five d orbitals.

00:31:26.901 --> 00:31:27.702
Five d orbitals.

00:31:27.702 --> 00:31:31.906
And you can do f on your own,
and it's an extremely fun thing

00:31:31.906 --> 00:31:33.141
to do at night.

00:31:33.141 --> 00:31:34.475
Late at night.

00:31:34.475 --> 00:31:38.613
Now, this gives
you the orbitals.

00:31:38.613 --> 00:31:39.647
You get them all.

00:31:39.647 --> 00:31:41.216
These are the orbitals.

00:31:41.216 --> 00:31:43.885
These are the beautiful,
beautiful orbitals

00:31:43.885 --> 00:31:46.554
that we have from
quantum mechanics

00:31:46.554 --> 00:31:48.756
describing an
electron in an atom.

00:31:48.756 --> 00:31:55.196
Which tells you everything about
where that electron can be,

00:31:55.196 --> 00:31:59.834
how it behaves, what
its energies are,

00:31:59.834 --> 00:32:03.338
and therefore literally why
atoms are what they are.

00:32:03.338 --> 00:32:05.240
That's what it tells us, right?

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:09.978
Because the next thing we need
to do is fill up our atoms.

00:32:09.978 --> 00:32:13.648
And know how electrons fill
atoms using these quantum

00:32:13.648 --> 00:32:15.049
numbers.

00:32:15.049 --> 00:32:16.684
And then relate
that to chemistry,

00:32:16.684 --> 00:32:19.254
and to the differences
between one atom and another,

00:32:19.254 --> 00:32:21.689
and explaining why
atoms are different.

00:32:21.689 --> 00:32:24.559
Remember, that was our goal.

00:32:24.559 --> 00:32:26.227
We needed to go
quantum to answer that.

00:32:29.264 --> 00:32:32.367
So we're getting closer,
but we're still not there.

00:32:32.367 --> 00:32:36.738
And one of the reasons is there
is a fourth quantum number.

00:32:36.738 --> 00:32:38.239
And this came out
of experiments--

00:32:38.239 --> 00:32:40.041
I thought that I
would just show you

00:32:40.041 --> 00:32:45.914
a very quick video, because it's
easier to see this in action.

00:32:45.914 --> 00:32:47.649
And so these are
experiments that

00:32:47.649 --> 00:32:51.185
were done by Stern
and Gerlach that

00:32:51.185 --> 00:32:53.855
showed something very
peculiar about atoms

00:32:53.855 --> 00:32:55.223
and their electrons.

00:32:55.223 --> 00:32:57.625
OK, so here I'm just
going to play it.

00:32:57.625 --> 00:32:58.927
It's got a really nice--

00:32:58.927 --> 00:32:59.661
oh, that's loud.

00:33:02.597 --> 00:33:03.665
Yeah?

00:33:03.665 --> 00:33:04.432
I don't mind that.

00:33:04.432 --> 00:33:04.933
That's good.

00:33:04.933 --> 00:33:06.401
So you can read the text here.

00:33:06.401 --> 00:33:07.635
OK, so they've got a magnet.

00:33:07.635 --> 00:33:10.038
A very strong magnet, right?

00:33:10.038 --> 00:33:11.506
Magnetic fields.

00:33:11.506 --> 00:33:13.708
And they're going to
shoot a classical magnet.

00:33:13.708 --> 00:33:14.842
There's a classical magnet.

00:33:14.842 --> 00:33:17.845
And you see it's going to
react to the magnetic field,

00:33:17.845 --> 00:33:20.615
and be pushed up.

00:33:20.615 --> 00:33:22.850
That magnetic could've
been oriented differently.

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:23.785
Like sideways, right?

00:33:23.785 --> 00:33:25.653
In which case it
doesn't feel this.

00:33:25.653 --> 00:33:27.121
And you could throw
a bunch of them

00:33:27.121 --> 00:33:30.491
randomly, with
random orientations,

00:33:30.491 --> 00:33:32.226
and that's what you would see.

00:33:32.226 --> 00:33:33.962
[JAZZY MUSIC]

00:33:33.962 --> 00:33:35.263
It's good music, too.

00:33:35.263 --> 00:33:36.397
But now-- watch this.

00:33:36.397 --> 00:33:37.966
I love the animation here.

00:33:37.966 --> 00:33:40.201
Look at this.

00:33:40.201 --> 00:33:43.638
And the bass, right?

00:33:43.638 --> 00:33:46.374
Those are what happens
to atoms with electrons.

00:33:46.374 --> 00:33:48.376
You'd shoot them
through a high field,

00:33:48.376 --> 00:33:51.145
and you don't get anything.

00:33:51.145 --> 00:33:52.680
You get two things.

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:54.148
Only two.

00:33:54.148 --> 00:33:56.951
[JAZZY MUSIC]

00:34:07.795 --> 00:34:10.098
I'm just playing it because
I really want to groove out

00:34:10.098 --> 00:34:13.534
and I'm debating--

00:34:13.534 --> 00:34:15.103
OK.

00:34:15.103 --> 00:34:17.237
All done.

00:34:17.237 --> 00:34:19.040
I was debating how
embarrassing it would be

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:20.541
if I just started grooving out.

00:34:23.844 --> 00:34:27.047
So this was Sterin and Gerlach,
who discovered, essentially,

00:34:27.047 --> 00:34:31.953
a fourth quantum number that
can have only two values.

00:34:31.953 --> 00:34:35.223
So they showed that this thing
that the atoms were doing

00:34:35.223 --> 00:34:37.225
was because of the electrons.

00:34:37.225 --> 00:34:40.128
And that the only
way you could explain

00:34:40.128 --> 00:34:42.964
what these electrons
were doing is if there

00:34:42.964 --> 00:34:45.099
was this thing called spin.

00:34:45.099 --> 00:34:47.034
And if you really need
to think classically--

00:34:47.034 --> 00:34:50.438
and it's dangerous to do
so, because then you'd

00:34:50.438 --> 00:34:53.074
forget that I could be all
the way over there, right?

00:34:53.074 --> 00:34:54.108
Right now.

00:34:54.108 --> 00:34:55.943
But if you really need
to think classically,

00:34:55.943 --> 00:34:57.645
you can think of
a charge spinning.

00:34:57.645 --> 00:35:00.481
It could be spinning
this way or that way.

00:35:00.481 --> 00:35:03.284
In a way, it helps
our classical needs.

00:35:03.284 --> 00:35:05.453
But it's so not
what's happening.

00:35:05.453 --> 00:35:09.757
But it does help our
classical desires, right?

00:35:09.757 --> 00:35:11.993
And that would give
you the two fields.

00:35:11.993 --> 00:35:12.960
Right?

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:16.097
But I would advise you just
to think about spin as simply

00:35:16.097 --> 00:35:18.800
a fourth quantum number.

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:22.303
And so we call
that one number 4.

00:35:22.303 --> 00:35:24.105
Not from that equation.

00:35:24.105 --> 00:35:26.674
Separate from those experiments.

00:35:26.674 --> 00:35:31.379
And that is-- we call
that the spin M sub

00:35:31.379 --> 00:35:35.283
s is the spin quantum number.

00:35:35.283 --> 00:35:39.187
And it's the fourth
one in our series.

00:35:39.187 --> 00:35:41.355
The fourth quantum number.

00:35:41.355 --> 00:35:43.191
And it has only two values--

00:35:43.191 --> 00:35:46.160
up or down.

00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:51.699
Or if you like, you could write
it as plus 1/2 or minus 1/2.

00:35:51.699 --> 00:35:55.903
Or you could write it
as up or down arrows.

00:35:55.903 --> 00:36:00.808
So many ways to distinguish
two things that are opposite.

00:36:00.808 --> 00:36:02.143
Spin up, spin down.

00:36:02.143 --> 00:36:04.445
Two values that this can have.

00:36:07.982 --> 00:36:08.950
So we're not ready yet.

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:09.851
We're still not ready.

00:36:09.851 --> 00:36:15.256
We're so close, but we're
still not there, right?

00:36:15.256 --> 00:36:17.525
Because we still don't
know, well-- can't I just--?

00:36:17.525 --> 00:36:18.059
How do I--?

00:36:18.059 --> 00:36:22.396
What are the rules
here going to be?

00:36:22.396 --> 00:36:24.632
For populating atoms.

00:36:24.632 --> 00:36:28.236
And this is where Pauli said
something very important,

00:36:28.236 --> 00:36:33.341
and made this incredibly
important statement.

00:36:33.341 --> 00:36:36.511
Which is that-- no particles
in the same system--

00:36:36.511 --> 00:36:40.815
read that as, no electrons
in the same atom--

00:36:40.815 --> 00:36:44.051
can have the same
quantum numbers.

00:36:44.051 --> 00:36:47.655
No electrons in the same atom
can have the same quantum

00:36:47.655 --> 00:36:48.723
numbers.

00:36:48.723 --> 00:36:51.792
And that immediately tells you--

00:36:51.792 --> 00:36:55.263
again, this has to do with the
nature of quantum mechanics--

00:36:55.263 --> 00:37:01.068
what that immediately tells
you is that two electrons--

00:37:01.068 --> 00:37:04.338
ho-ho, this is big!

00:37:04.338 --> 00:37:08.209
Two electrons-- I'm gonna write,
two electrons, and watch this--

00:37:08.209 --> 00:37:09.477
I'm going all caps--

00:37:09.477 --> 00:37:16.817
only two electrons
can occupy an orbital.

00:37:20.955 --> 00:37:21.489
An orbital.

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:27.929
Or- bi- tal.

00:37:30.565 --> 00:37:35.503
That called the Pauli
exclusion principle.

00:37:35.503 --> 00:37:36.003
OK.

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:41.142
So we're going to use
all of this information

00:37:41.142 --> 00:37:45.513
to fill stuff up, but I
don't want to do that yet.

00:37:45.513 --> 00:37:48.282
On Wednesday, we're
filling up the table,

00:37:48.282 --> 00:37:49.650
and we're making
our first solid.

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:51.419
I figure it's our
three week anniversary.

00:37:51.419 --> 00:37:53.955
I know you've all been
looking forward to it.

00:37:53.955 --> 00:37:55.456
I've been thinking
about it a lot.

00:37:55.456 --> 00:37:59.460
And I think-- that makes
me want to throw t-shirts.

