00:38:37.398 --> 00:38:37.898
Literally.

00:38:37.898 --> 00:38:40.968
We are not only making a bond,
but we're making a solid,

00:38:40.968 --> 00:38:42.003
and talking about solids.

00:38:42.003 --> 00:38:43.771
So that's going to
be very exciting.

00:38:43.771 --> 00:38:46.440
And we're going to start
by filling elements.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:50.778
But before we do that, I
want to explain something

00:38:50.778 --> 00:38:54.382
very important, which is--

00:38:54.382 --> 00:38:56.951
what's happening with energy?

00:38:56.951 --> 00:38:57.518
Right?

00:38:57.518 --> 00:39:01.088
We did just see
something dramatic.

00:39:01.088 --> 00:39:04.358
As you go from Bohr to quantum,
something very dramatic

00:39:04.358 --> 00:39:09.063
happens with where, right?

00:39:09.063 --> 00:39:11.632
And we know something
else very dramatic

00:39:11.632 --> 00:39:15.036
happens, which is right here.

00:39:15.036 --> 00:39:18.439
You know, that's Bohr over
there- n equals 1, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:18.439 --> 00:39:23.077
Bohr is 1, 2, 3, right?

00:39:23.077 --> 00:39:26.414
Now it's like-- oh no,
wait, it's 1 and with 1s.

00:39:26.414 --> 00:39:29.550
2-- I can put two electrons
in there, thanks to Pauli.

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:31.852
I know I can put two, because
I have all the quantum

00:39:31.852 --> 00:39:34.021
numbers in here the
same except spin, which

00:39:34.021 --> 00:39:36.457
could be up or down, right?

00:39:36.457 --> 00:39:40.728
N equals 1, l equals 0, m sub
l equals 0, spin is up or down.

00:39:40.728 --> 00:39:41.562
Two electrons.

00:39:41.562 --> 00:39:45.032
But look, I'm now
here at n equals 2,

00:39:45.032 --> 00:39:47.034
going from the Bohr
model to quantum.

00:39:47.034 --> 00:39:52.106
I've got this and I've
got three p orbitals.

00:39:52.106 --> 00:39:54.642
So now all of a sudden
I've got eight electrons

00:39:54.642 --> 00:39:57.445
that can go into
that quantum number.

00:39:57.445 --> 00:40:01.916
That's very different
than Bohr, right?

00:40:01.916 --> 00:40:05.119
And that's called degeneracy,
because you're taking something

00:40:05.119 --> 00:40:07.388
and you're saying, there
are multiple variations

00:40:07.388 --> 00:40:09.256
that have the same n.

00:40:09.256 --> 00:40:10.991
But there's
something else that I

00:40:10.991 --> 00:40:14.528
want to explain right now
that is can be understood very

00:40:14.528 --> 00:40:16.564
physically and intuitively.

00:40:16.564 --> 00:40:19.333
And that is that
the motivation-- one

00:40:19.333 --> 00:40:21.702
of the big motivations
of this was that we

00:40:21.702 --> 00:40:24.372
needed more than one electron.

00:40:24.372 --> 00:40:28.109
Our atoms are not all
hydrogen and ionized atoms.

00:40:28.109 --> 00:40:31.011
They are just neutral atoms
going up above hydrogen.

00:40:31.011 --> 00:40:34.882
So what happens when
we go multi electron?

00:40:34.882 --> 00:40:36.984
And there's two effects
that I want to explain.

00:40:36.984 --> 00:40:39.153
Here's what happens
to this picture.

00:40:39.153 --> 00:40:40.855
Those are the one
electron levels, right?

00:40:40.855 --> 00:40:42.656
There's Bohr over there.

00:40:42.656 --> 00:40:46.360
This is what you really
get in terms of energy.

00:40:46.360 --> 00:40:46.861
Right?

00:40:46.861 --> 00:40:48.763
This is energy along
the vertical axis.

00:40:48.763 --> 00:40:52.233
And there's two reasons,
two very important reasons,

00:40:52.233 --> 00:40:54.068
why these are so different.

00:40:54.068 --> 00:40:56.103
One is called
shielding, and the other

00:40:56.103 --> 00:40:57.338
is called orbital penetration.

00:40:57.338 --> 00:40:59.206
And I want to talk
about both of them

00:40:59.206 --> 00:41:03.511
in the last seven
minutes of class.

00:41:03.511 --> 00:41:05.012
I'm going over here.

00:41:08.983 --> 00:41:11.652
All right, so let's talk about--

00:41:11.652 --> 00:41:12.720
let's do shielding first.

00:41:15.723 --> 00:41:19.927
Shielding is something
that you can kind of

00:41:19.927 --> 00:41:23.497
get a very good sense for it
if you just look at what's

00:41:23.497 --> 00:41:25.132
happening in these atoms.

00:41:28.102 --> 00:41:35.576
So in shielding--
let's take an example.

00:41:35.576 --> 00:41:40.314
I'm going to give you an
atom with 15 protons and 15

00:41:40.314 --> 00:41:41.749
electrons.

00:41:41.749 --> 00:41:43.918
What atom is it?

00:41:43.918 --> 00:41:46.253
Anybody know?

00:41:46.253 --> 00:41:47.421
Phosphorus.

00:41:47.421 --> 00:41:50.458
OK, so I'm going to take an
example of the phosphorus atom,

00:41:50.458 --> 00:41:53.027
OK?

00:41:53.027 --> 00:41:59.733
And I've got 15 positive
charges in the nucleus.

00:41:59.733 --> 00:42:00.868
15.

00:42:00.868 --> 00:42:03.437
Right?

00:42:03.437 --> 00:42:05.873
Now, 15 protons.

00:42:05.873 --> 00:42:09.510
Now I've got my
levels, and it gets

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:14.381
very difficult to draw levels
as probability distributions.

00:42:14.381 --> 00:42:16.484
So I'm going to draw them
as rings, even though we

00:42:16.484 --> 00:42:17.918
know they're not, right?

00:42:17.918 --> 00:42:18.986
These are my orbitals.

00:42:18.986 --> 00:42:23.290
But I've got like an electron
here, OK, an electron there.

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:23.791
All right.

00:42:23.791 --> 00:42:27.795
So I've got two electrons
in the n equals 1--

00:42:27.795 --> 00:42:29.763
n equals 1 orbital.

00:42:29.763 --> 00:42:34.201
So that's the 1s orbital,
right there on the bottom.

00:42:34.201 --> 00:42:36.103
But if I go out--

00:42:36.103 --> 00:42:39.940
OK, so I'm going to go out now.

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:42.776
To n equals 2.

00:42:42.776 --> 00:42:50.451
Well, now I've got
1, 2, 3, 4, and--

00:42:50.451 --> 00:42:52.753
oh boy-- put one here.

00:42:52.753 --> 00:42:57.191
5, 6, 7, 8.

00:42:57.191 --> 00:43:01.595
Because here-- remember, I've
got l equals 0 and l equals 1,

00:43:01.595 --> 00:43:04.765
and as we just said, because
n sub s can be up or down,

00:43:04.765 --> 00:43:07.334
I've got eight electrons here.

00:43:07.334 --> 00:43:10.437
So here, I've got n equals
1, here I've got n equals 2.

00:43:10.437 --> 00:43:11.772
That's eight electrons.

00:43:11.772 --> 00:43:13.440
And then out here--

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:16.310
oh, now it gets really
interesting-- because for n

00:43:16.310 --> 00:43:18.946
equals 3, how many
electrons do I have left?

00:43:18.946 --> 00:43:23.717
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:43:23.717 --> 00:43:25.819
And you can already
see what's happening.

00:43:25.819 --> 00:43:27.855
So remember, Rutherford
told us this.

00:43:27.855 --> 00:43:31.158
All this charge is in a
tiny little point here.

00:43:31.158 --> 00:43:36.063
You wouldn't even see it if
we were drawing this to scale.

00:43:36.063 --> 00:43:39.433
But what are these
electrons seeing?

00:43:39.433 --> 00:43:40.734
What are the electrons seeing?

00:43:40.734 --> 00:43:43.537
Well, these inner two
electrons are just

00:43:43.537 --> 00:43:45.339
seeing this huge
amount of charge.

00:43:45.339 --> 00:43:47.474
They are happy.

00:43:47.474 --> 00:43:49.376
Low energy.

00:43:49.376 --> 00:43:50.044
Right?

00:43:50.044 --> 00:43:51.712
The lower your
energy, the happier.

00:43:51.712 --> 00:43:53.647
You're in a lower
energy, ground state.

00:43:53.647 --> 00:43:57.851
Remember-- more lower
energy, more happiness.

00:43:57.851 --> 00:43:59.153
More bound.

00:43:59.153 --> 00:44:00.654
Right?

00:44:00.654 --> 00:44:05.793
So those electrons down
there are enjoying.

00:44:05.793 --> 00:44:07.861
But these out here--

00:44:07.861 --> 00:44:08.429
you see?

00:44:08.429 --> 00:44:10.731
These electrons.

00:44:10.731 --> 00:44:12.766
Those electrons in here--

00:44:12.766 --> 00:44:17.805
these shield the outer
electrons, these shield

00:44:17.805 --> 00:44:23.744
outer electrons
by two electrons.

00:44:23.744 --> 00:44:24.411
Right?

00:44:24.411 --> 00:44:27.481
Because they're
kind of in the way.

00:44:27.481 --> 00:44:30.117
It's like you've got this
negative charge that's

00:44:30.117 --> 00:44:33.053
in between me and the
positive charge, right?

00:44:33.053 --> 00:44:35.456
And so if I'm out
here at n equals 2,

00:44:35.456 --> 00:44:42.162
I don't see 15 positive
charges there anymore.

00:44:42.162 --> 00:44:44.865
I see 13-- ish.

00:44:44.865 --> 00:44:46.900
That's shielding.

00:44:46.900 --> 00:44:48.369
But now this one--

00:44:48.369 --> 00:44:53.307
this has the 2s and
the 2p electrons.

00:44:53.307 --> 00:44:55.976
It's got eight electrons
there, and those

00:44:55.976 --> 00:44:58.779
are shielding another eight.

00:44:58.779 --> 00:45:03.651
Those are shielding
another eight protons.

00:45:07.388 --> 00:45:09.623
To things outside
of them, right?

00:45:09.623 --> 00:45:11.225
Shielding doesn't work going in.

00:45:11.225 --> 00:45:17.965
If I'm this electron and I look
out, all I see are repulsive--

00:45:17.965 --> 00:45:19.967
I see repulsiveness.

00:45:19.967 --> 00:45:20.601
In a good way--

00:45:20.601 --> 00:45:21.869
I'm sure they're friends.

00:45:21.869 --> 00:45:26.206
But two negative charges, right?

00:45:26.206 --> 00:45:27.841
So I'm only looking in.

00:45:27.841 --> 00:45:30.444
Looking in, shielded.

00:45:30.444 --> 00:45:32.980
Looking in, shielded.

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:36.316
So these ones have
sort of an effective--

00:45:36.316 --> 00:45:38.118
z effective.

00:45:38.118 --> 00:45:41.789
You know, that could
be something like five.

00:45:41.789 --> 00:45:44.258
This is not the z of
the atom, but it's

00:45:44.258 --> 00:45:46.760
sort of like those very
outer electrons don't

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:49.063
see the whole proton cloud.

00:45:49.063 --> 00:45:49.830
They get shielded.

00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:52.599
And that's a very
important part of what

00:45:52.599 --> 00:45:56.103
happens to orbitals when you
put a lot of electrons in them.

00:45:56.103 --> 00:45:58.505
And there's one more
thing that happens,

00:45:58.505 --> 00:46:03.343
and it goes back to this plot
that I showed you before,

00:46:03.343 --> 00:46:08.449
which is that the
orbitals themselves

00:46:08.449 --> 00:46:14.555
are very different than just
like a straight line from Bohr.

00:46:14.555 --> 00:46:17.091
They have these
shapes to them, right?

00:46:17.091 --> 00:46:25.532
So the second thing that happens
is that if I have that same s--

00:46:25.532 --> 00:46:28.836
this is s, so this
would now be the 1s.

00:46:28.836 --> 00:46:30.003
And now I'm going to put--

00:46:30.003 --> 00:46:33.507
remember that 2s
looked like this.

00:46:33.507 --> 00:46:34.742
And it had the node there.

00:46:34.742 --> 00:46:35.342
Remember that?

00:46:35.342 --> 00:46:36.944
Ho-ho.

00:46:36.944 --> 00:46:39.580
That was 2s.

00:46:39.580 --> 00:46:44.184
But now we're going
to put the 2p there.

00:46:44.184 --> 00:46:47.488
May not be drawn to scale.

00:46:47.488 --> 00:46:49.089
OK, here's the 2p.

00:46:53.093 --> 00:46:56.096
Something very, very
interesting is happening here.

00:46:56.096 --> 00:46:58.932
And again, it has to do with
what electrons are seeing.

00:46:58.932 --> 00:47:02.269
Because the 2s electron
has a probability--

00:47:02.269 --> 00:47:05.239
remember, this is the
probability normalized,

00:47:05.239 --> 00:47:08.642
so we put in r-squared
there, right?

00:47:08.642 --> 00:47:11.945
That probability-- all that
means is if I integrate over

00:47:11.945 --> 00:47:13.080
everything, it's 1.

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:14.548
It's got to be somewhere.

00:47:14.548 --> 00:47:19.686
But look, the 2s electron can be
really deep inside the shells.

00:47:19.686 --> 00:47:22.689
It can be in there.

00:47:22.689 --> 00:47:26.493
Literally-- look, it's
even inside the 1s.

00:47:26.493 --> 00:47:31.799
So it gets some of that
15-proton action, right?

00:47:31.799 --> 00:47:35.102
Orbital penetration
leads to massive changes.

00:47:35.102 --> 00:47:37.404
Orbital penetration is
what this is called,

00:47:37.404 --> 00:47:40.741
and it allows some of the--

00:47:40.741 --> 00:47:42.810
but the p doesn't have that.

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:44.378
It goes in a little,
but the p doesn't

00:47:44.378 --> 00:47:46.180
have that peak, all
the way in there,

00:47:46.180 --> 00:47:48.182
where it can see all
that positive charge.

00:47:48.182 --> 00:47:52.886
And that is why these
two things are different.

00:47:52.886 --> 00:47:53.554
You see?

00:47:53.554 --> 00:47:57.558
Because the 2s now,
can be closer sometimes

00:47:57.558 --> 00:48:00.127
to those 15 protons, right?

00:48:00.127 --> 00:48:03.330
And so it lowers the
energy, making it happier.

00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:05.532
It finds a lower happy place.

00:48:05.532 --> 00:48:06.033
OK?

00:48:06.033 --> 00:48:11.772
So we're going to use this,
and on Wednesday, we're

00:48:11.772 --> 00:48:14.308
going to take these things and
we're going to fill them up,

00:48:14.308 --> 00:48:16.877
and we're going to
make our first solid.

00:48:16.877 --> 00:48:19.813
Have a very good
rest of your day.

